# A leader chord resolves on its second key, however fast it is typed:
# `space e` focuses the sidebar (NvChad's NvimTreeFocus) and `space f f`
# opens the file picker with no wait between the keys (Neovim resolves
# the mapping on the key, not on `timeoutlen`). The `e` must not run as
# the end-of-word motion — the cursor stays on column 1 — and Esc on a
# pending leader cancels it without opening which-key. `<C-n>` is the
# toggle (the sidebar's ` ` chevron is the tell). The runner settles and
# expires chords after every `key` step, so the fast typing is a `type`
# step — its keys go in back to back.
write b.zig "const std = @import(\"std\");\n"
open b.zig
command editor.use_vim
expect screen contains " "
expect screen contains "Col 1 "
type " e"
expect screen contains "TREE"
expect screen contains "Col 1 "
expect screen lacks "┌ <leader>"
key esc
expect screen contains "NORMAL"
key ctrl+n
expect screen lacks " "
key ctrl+n
expect screen contains " "
expect screen contains "TREE"
key esc
expect screen contains "Col 1 "
type " ff"
expect screen contains "┌ Open file"
key esc
expect screen lacks "┌ Open file"
key space
key esc
wait 700
expect screen lacks "┌ <leader>"
expect screen contains "Col 1 "
